<section xml:id="section-primes">
  <title>Rings and Prime Factorization</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-prime-ideals">
    <p>
      I said that <m>\ZZ</m> was the archetypical ring, and the most
      important and fascinating structure in the integers is the
      sequence of prime numbers. In the previous section on polynomial
      rings, I've already started talking about factoring polynomials,
      which obviously bring up ideas of prime factorization. So now,
      in this section, I'll bring that all together and talk about how
      primes are dealt with in general (commutative) ring theory. 
    </p>
    <p>
      In the integers, primes are numbers, elements of the ring. For a
      small number of rings, this still works. But, in the deveopment
      of ring theory, it was realized that focusing on numbers, ring
      elements, doesn't actually work. Instead, we need to focus on
      ideals. 
    </p>
    <p>
      In the integers, all ideas are principal. That is, they are
      generated by a single number. So, instead of a prime number
      <m>p</m>, I can talk about the matching ideal <m>\langle p
      \rangle</m>. Then, instead of talking about how <m>10</m>
      factors into two prime, I can ask about how the ideal <m>\langle
      10 \rangle</m> factors in ideals <m>\langle 2 \rangle</m> and
      <m>\langle 5 \rangle </m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      All this isn't strictly necessary for the integers, but it turns
      out to be the way forward for general rings. So, let me get to
      the definitions.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>I \subset R</m> be a proper ideal in a ring. Assume
          that <m>a,b \in R</m>. If <m>ab \in I</m> implies that either
          <m>a \in I</m> or <m>b \in I</m>, then <m>I</m> is called a
          <term>prime ideal</term>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The prime ideals are going to be the <sq>prime numbers</sq> of
      ring theory. Let me go back to the integers to convince you that
      this definition makes sense.  
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An ideal <m>I</m> in <m>\ZZ</m> is prime if and ony if <m>I =
          \langle p \rangle</m> for some prime number <m>p</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          First, I'll assume <m>I = \langle p \rangle</m>. Then, to show
          that this is prime, I'll assume <m>a,b \in \ZZ</m> and <m>ab
          \in \langle p \rangle</m>. This means <m>ab</m> is a multiple
          of <m>p</m>, which I can write this way. 
          <me>
            ab = np 
          </me>
          Now the prime divides the right of this equation, so the
          prime also divides the left. And, from the properties of the
          integer, this means that the prime must be a factor of
          either <m>a</m> or <m>b</m>. But that means that one of
          <m>a</m> or <m>b</m> is a multiple of the prime<mdash/> <m>a
          \in \langle p \rangle</m> or <m>b \in \langle p \rangle</m>.
          That's the definition of a prime ideal, so <m>\langle p
          \rangle</m> is prime. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Now for the other direction, I'll assume <m>I</m> is a prime
          ideal in the integers. Take any element <m>n \in I</m>. This
          element has a prime decomposition.
          <me>
            n = p_1 p_2 \ldots p_j
          </me>
          But I can use the property of prime ideals. Since <m>n</m> is
          this product, at least one of the factors must be in the
          ideal. So there is a prime number <m>p_i \in I</m>. This means
          <m>\langle p_i \rangle \subset I</m>. 
        </p>
        <p>
          I'll claim that this is an equality of sets, not just a
          subset. If there were some number <m>m</m> coprime to
          <m>p</m> in the ideal, then again, using the properties of
          the integers, I can write <m>1</m> as a linear combination
          of <m>m</m> and <m>p</m>. This means <m> 1 \in I</m>, which
          leads to <m>I = \ZZ</m>. Since a prime ideal must be proper,
          this can't happen. So the claim is established by
          contradiction and the proof is complete. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      So, instead of prime numbers, I have prime ideals. For full
      generality, I will work with ideals, and there will be examples
      where I need ideals to capture all the primes. However,
      sometimes I can still talk about <sq>prime number</sq> in a
      ring. Here is that definition.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>p \in R</m>. The elemtn <m>p</m> is called
          <term>prime</term> if the principle idea <m>\langle p
          \rangle</m> is a prime ideal. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The important fact about prime number is that other numbers can
      be factored using the prime numbers. The same should be true for
      ideals.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>I,J \subset R</m> be ideals in a ring. The
          <term>product of the ideals</term>, written <m>IJ</m>,
          consists of all ring elements which are sums of products of
          elements of the two elements.
          <me>
            IJ = \left\{ \sum_k i_k j_k | i_k \in I, j_k \in J \right\}
          </me>
          Having defined a product, <term>factors</term> and
          <term>multiples</term> make for ideals and carry their usual
          sense using this definition of product. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>I \subset R</m> be an ideal in a ring. A <term>prime
          decomposision</term> of the idea is a set of prime ideals (not
          necessarily unique) <m>P_i</m> such that 
          <me>
            I = P_1 P_2 \ldots P_k 
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      In the integers, we know that all numbers uniquely factor into
      prime (up to multiplication by units). For the integers, that's
      equivalent to saying that all ideals uniquely factor into prime
      ideals (conveniently, I don't even need to say <sq>up to
      units</sq> any more). But is this true for other rings? It is
      not.  Here is the definition. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A domain <m>R</m> is called a <term>unique factorization
          domain</term> if every ideal in <m>R</m> uniquely factors into
          a product of (non-necessarily distinct) prime ideals. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Note that this definition is only for domains: a ring with zero
      divisors is not even considered here. 
    </p>
    <p>
      With this definition, we get to a major problem in ring theory:
      which domains have unique factorizations? This turns out to be a
      pretty interesting question. I eventually want to give you a
      bunch of examples to demonstrate rings which do and do not have
      unique factorization. But there is a bit of work to do before we
      get there. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-norms">
    <title>Norms</title>
    <p>
      Before figuring out unique factorization, I have to have a sense
      of how to find prime number in one of these new rings. This is
      easier said than done. How do I find the prime in the integers?
      I know the prime are the positive number with no non-trivial
      factors, but other than that fact, the primes don't have any
      other pattern. This is, famously, what makes the primes so
      difficult and so interesting. To find primes, you have to
      calculate factors. We don't have a formula for all the prime: we
      find them by factoring (along with various related tricks that
      can limit the search of factors). 
    </p>
    <p>
      Notably, we look for them in order, starting with the smallest
      primes: <m>2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, \ldots </m>. For this to
      generalizz, I need a sense of order: which are the smallest
      elements of a ring. In full generality, this is very hard to do.
      But I can give you a definition for a set of rings that I have
      already define, which are the rings that we will work with for
      all the examples. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>D</m> b a square-free integer and consider the ring
          <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{D}]</m>. The <term>norm</term> on this ring is
          the following function. 
          <me>
            N(a + \sqrt{D}) = a^2 - Db^2
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The norm is a function that can be used to determine which are
      the <sq>smallest</sq> elements of the ring <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{D}]</m>.
      In some sense, it generalizes the absolute value on the ordinary
      integers. It is a multiplicative function: the norm of a product
      is the product of the two norms (a property also shared by
      absolute value.) This is important, since is means that the norm
      of elements of a product cannot exceed (in absolute value) the
      norm of the product. 
    </p>
    <p>
      There is another property of this norm, which I won't prove
      here, but which is very useul. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The units in <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{D}]</m> are precisely the elements
          that have norm <m>\pm 1</m> 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-guassian-integerss">
    <title>Primes in the Guassian Integers</title>
    <p>
      So I want to start calculating the primes in <m>\ZZ[i]</m>.
      First, what is the norm? Here <m>D = -1</m>, so the normal is 
      <me>
        N(a + bi) = a^2 + b^2
      </me>
      Note that this norm is always positive, which simplifies the
      investigation. Only <m>0</m> has norm 0, which is always true.
      Next, the units will have norm 1. The only way to get norm 1 is
      to have one of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be 0 and the other 1.
      Therefore, the units are just <m>\ZZ[i]^* = \{1, -1, i, -i\}</m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Now I can talk about primes. I'm going to talk about prime
      number here (for reasons I'll prove later) instead of the more
      general prime ideals. The elements of norm 2 must be primes.
      What are these elements? I can get a norm two by having the
      coefficients both be <m>\pm 1</m> and that is the only way. I
      get this set. 
      <me>  
        \{1 + i, 1 - i, -1 + i, -1 - i \} 
      </me>
      These are all prime, and they are the first four primes.
      However, they aren't really <sq>four</sq> primes. Here are three
      calculations.
      <md>
        <mrow>
          -1(1 + i) = - 1 - i 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          i(1 + i) = i - 1 = -1 + i 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          -i(1 + i) = -i - (-1) = 1 - i
        </mrow>
      </md>
      One I have the prime <m>(1 + i)</m>, all three other
      <sq>primes</sq> can be constructed by multiplication up to
      units. Primes are only unique up to units (in the usual
      integers, <m>7</m> and <m>-7</m> are considered the same prime
      number.) More importantly, multiplication by a unit doesn't
      change the principal ideal: all four of these generate exactly
      the same prime ideal. Therefore, there is only really one prime
      of norm 1, it just can vary up to units. 
    </p>
    <p>
      I move on to higher norms. Norm three is impossible: <m>a^2 +
      b^2 = 3</m> is not true for any integer inputs. So the next norm
      to consider is four. Here are all the elements of norm four.
      <me>
        \{2, -2, 2i, -2i\}
      </me>
      Some of these might be prime and some might be products of the
      previous primes. How do I check? Well, the most obvious way is
      the same way I check for the integers: look at the products of
      smaller primes. If I investigate the products of the smaller
      primes, I can find the following identity. 
      <me>
        2 = (1+i)(1-i) = -i(1+i)(1+i)
      </me>
      This is a factorization of the number 2 (more formally, the
      Guassian integer <m>(2 + 0i)</m> into primes. In the Guassian
      integers, 2 is no longer a prime. When a prime from <m>\ZZ</m>
      is no longer prime in a larger ring, we say that the prime
      <term>splits</term> in the larger ring. In the Guassian
      integers, 2 splits. 
    </p>
    <p>
      What about the other three? Well, the other three are all 2
      multiplied by a unit and primes are only unique up to units. So
      none of the elements of norm 4 are prime: they all factor as the
      square of the prime <m>(1 + i)</m>, up to multiplication by
      units. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The next norm is five. There are eight elements of norm five. 
      <me>
        \{\pm 1 \pm 2i, \pm 2 \pm i\}
      </me>
      Up to multiplication by units, there are only two elements here. 
      <me>
        \{1 + 2i, 1 - 2i}
      </me>
      I claim that both of these are prime. Why? Well, I only have one
      prime so far, which has norm 2. The square of that prime has
      norm 4, and its cube has norm 8. I can never get a norm of 5
      this way, so an element of norm 5 cannot be the product of
      smaller non-units. They must both be prime. 
    </p>
    <p>
      There are no elements of norm 6 or 7: these numbers cannot be
      produced by the norm formul <m>a^2 + b^2</m>. So the next norm
      is norm 8. Up to units there is only one element of norm 8. 
      <me>
        \{2 + 2i}
      </me>
      This factors pretty clearly: <m>2 + 2i = 2(1 + i)</m>, and I
      already know now 2 factors. Therefore <m>2 + 2i = -i(1 +
      i)^3</m>. This is not a prime.
    </p>
    <p>
      I can keep doing this for a while. Here is a table of the
      progress through the next few norms. Everything is unique up to
      multiplication by units. I skip over the norms which have no
      elements. Note that any compositie integer, such as 4, cannot be
      prime in any larger ring since the factorization in the integers
      still holds. 
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{array}{c| c c c c c c c }
        \text{Norm} \amp \text{Elements} \amp \text{Prime?} \\
        \hline
        9 \amp 3 + 0i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        10 \amp 3 + i \amp \text{No} \\
        10 \amp 3 - i \amp \text{No} \\
        13 \amp 3 + 2i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        13 \amp 3 - 2i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        16 \amp 4 + 0i \amp \text{No} \\
        17 \amp 4 + i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        17 \amp 4 - i \amp \text{Yes} \\
        18 \amp 3 + 3i \amp \text{No} \\
        20 \amp 4 + 2i \amp \text{No} \\ 
        20 \amp 4 - 2i \amp \text{No} \\ 
        25 \amp 5 + 0i \amp \text{No} \\
        25 \amp 4 + 3i \amp \text{No} \\
        25 \amp 4 - 3i \amp \text{No} 
      \end{array}
    </me>
    <p>
      Note that we get another split prime: 5 is no longer prime. Here
      is the factorization: <m>5 = (2 + i)(2 - i)</m>. I could keep
      going, but hopefully there is enough here. If there is enoguh, I
      could start to ask: is there some pattern here? If you stare
      long enough, perhaps after calcluating a bit more, you may be
      able to guess the following proposition. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Up to multiplication by units, the primes in the Guassian
          integers fit one of the two following patterns. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              <m>a + bi = p + 0i</m> where <m>p</m> is a usual prime
              in the integers which is congruent to 3 modulo 4.   
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>a^2 + b^2</m> is prime as an element of the normal
              integers.  
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
</section>
